---
/**
 * The arena — build steps 0 to 5 of `docs/cat-boss-gdd.md` §12.
 *
 * **Step 0**: wire the toggle in the cat's HUD (rendered by SiteCat.astro) to an
 * instant swap. No transition, no fade — the §14 ink curtain is deliberately the
 * *last* thing built, and this instant path stays as its permanent fallback.
 *
 * **Step 1**: Claim + Scrub. Hold the cursor still on a block the cat has taken and
 * it comes back to you.
 *
 * **Step 2**: Pounce. The cat leaves the footer, stalks the cursor, and interrupts a
 * scrub it judges worth interrupting — telegraph, leap, recover.
 *
 * **Step 3**: Treats. Click to spend one of the treats you found while browsing; the
 * cat abandons everything to go and eat it, and cannot pounce while it does.
 *
 * **Step 4**: The loop closes — a 3px territory bar on the top edge, two endings that
 * mean different things, and a cat that talks. Winning marks its ear for the session.
 *
 * **Step 5**: Replayability. The cat picks a **stance** per fight (§9.3) — same verbs,
 * different opponent — and treats stop being interchangeable: which pages you read decides
 * what your five shapes actually *do* (§9.5).
 *
 * What all of it does to the page, and nothing more: adds a class and one custom
 * property to some existing elements. Claims never affect layout and never hide
 * anything (§11), so text stays selectable and every link stays clickable while the
 * cat "holds" the block around it.
 *
 * The shape of the loop, now that all three exist: **a pounce always beats a scrub it
 * commits to** (it lands at ~84% of the hold), so ground is taken where the cat isn't
 * — and a treat is how you *make* somewhere the cat isn't. Ammo comes only from having
 * browsed the site (pillar 5), so a visitor who opens a fight cold has none, and the
 * tab bar is the tutorial.
 *
 * Off is the only state a page can load in. Nothing is written to storage.
 */
---

{/* The curtain (§14). Full-screen, on top of everything, and inert: a click during the
    transition is swallowed rather than landing on a half-claimed page. Hidden except
    during the ~1.7s it is running, so it costs a page that never opens a fight nothing
    but an empty element. */}
<canvas id="cat-curtain" transition:persist="cat-curtain" aria-hidden="true" hidden></canvas>

{/* Territory (§6): 3px on the top edge, no numbers.
    The cat pushes up from the bottom of the page, so the top edge is *your* edge, and
    the filled part is your share. A percentage would invite optimising a thing that is
    supposed to be felt — and there is nowhere on a portfolio for a scoreboard. */}
<div id="cat-territory" transition:persist="cat-territory" aria-hidden="true" hidden>
  <span class="territory-mine"></span>
</div>

{/* What the cat says (§8). Anchored above the cat and follows it — never centre-screen,
    which would cover the portfolio, which is the actual product. `aria-hidden` like the
    rest of the cat: nothing here carries information the player needs. */}
<div id="cat-ribbon" transition:persist="cat-ribbon" aria-hidden="true" hidden></div>

{/* A thrown treat. Its own element rather than SiteCat's `#cat-treat`: the shapes
    live in a shared <defs> block, so this reuses the art by reference without
    borrowing any of that element's state — no clobbering the treat a page is
    currently hiding, and no interference with the credit SiteCat owes it.

    `pointer-events: none` for the same reason the other one has it: a decorative
    treat has no business eating a click meant for a link. */}
<div id="cat-throw" transition:persist="cat-throw" aria-hidden="true" hidden>
  <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" aria-hidden="true">
    <use href="#treat-fish"></use>
  </svg>
</div>

{/* The scrub ring: a cursor-sized dial that fills while you hold still. Fixed,
    aria-hidden and pointer-events: none — it must never be the thing under the
    cursor, or it would eat the hover it is reporting on. */}
<div id="cat-scrub" transition:persist="cat-scrub" aria-hidden="true" hidden>
  <svg viewBox="0 0 40 40" width="40" height="40">
    <circle class="scrub-track" cx="20" cy="20" r="17" fill="none" stroke-width="2.5"></circle>
    <circle
      class="scrub-fill"
      cx="20"
      cy="20"
      r="17"
      fill="none"
      stroke-width="2.5"
      stroke-linecap="round"
      transform="rotate(-90 20 20)"></circle>
  </svg>
</div>

<style>
  /* ---- a claimed element ----
     §5.1 asked for "desaturated, tilted, paw watermark". Built and looked at, and
     the first two do nothing here: this page is cream and near-black, so
     `saturate(0.35)` is invisible on it, and 0.5° of tilt is a rounding error. A
     claim you cannot see is not a claim — there is nothing to scrub *toward*, which
     is the fun hypothesis failing for a reason that has nothing to do with the
     mechanic. What reads on a monochrome page is a wash and an edge:

     - `box-shadow: inset` paints a flat tint over the element's whole box, above
       its background and **below its text**, so the block is visibly taken without
       the words getting harder to read.
     - `outline` draws the boundary, and — the reason it is allowed here at all —
       outlines are painted, never laid out. Same guarantee §11 wanted from
       "transform and filter only": nothing moves, nothing is hidden.

     The wash is 7%, not the 12% it read best at: 12% put `.rail` — small, mono,
     `--color-muted` — at 4.15:1 against its own tinted background, under AA's 4.5,
     and §11 makes that a gate rather than a trade. So the edge does the shouting
     and the wash only murmurs. The tilt stays as a garnish, capped in `arena.ts`. */
  :global(.cat-claimed) {
    transform: rotate(var(--claim-tilt, 0deg)) scale(0.994);
    box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 100vmax color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-accent) 7%, transparent);
    outline: 2px dashed color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-accent) 62%, transparent);
    outline-offset: 3px;
    /* the pointer is the tool, so a claim says it is a target */
    cursor: crosshair;
  }
  /* The moment it comes back to you: the wash drains out rather than blinking off.
     Kept off under reduced motion like everything else, though the arena itself
     does not open there yet. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    :global(.cat-freed) {
      animation: cat-freed 0.42s ease-out;
    }
    @keyframes cat-freed {
      from {
        box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 100vmax color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-accent) 26%, transparent);
      }
      to {
        box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 100vmax transparent;
      }
    }
  }

  /* While a fight is on, the root clips horizontally. Belt and braces beside
     MAX_TILT: `clip` (not `hidden`) creates no scroll container, so the sticky
     header and every anchor jump still work. An easter egg does not get to give
     the page a horizontal scrollbar. */
  :global(html.cat-arena-on) {
    overflow-x: clip;
    /*
     * Crosshair = you can put a treat here. It is the whole affordance for §5.4, and
     * it costs nothing: links and buttons keep `cursor: pointer` from the UA
     * stylesheet, because a declaration on the element beats an inherited one. So the
     * cursor draws the one boundary the player needs — **crosshair throws, pointer
     * navigates** — with no new UI and nothing added to the page.
     *
     * It matters more than it looks. A portfolio is mostly links, and a click on one
     * still navigates (§11 promises the page keeps working), which ends the fight. The
     * cursor is what turns that from a mystery into a rule.
     */
    cursor: crosshair;
  }

  /* ---- the cat, while the arena has it (§5.3) ----
     `SiteCat.astro` owns this element; the arena borrows it via `cat:standdown` and
     writes only its transform. These rules are the phases, and they are all on the
     inner `.cat-svg` so they compose with the positioning transform rather than
     fighting it.

     `pointer-events: none` on the body is load-bearing twice over: a boss is not a
     pet, so it must not answer a boop mid-fight — and a cat parked on the cursor
     would otherwise be what `elementFromPoint` returns, which would silently make
     scrubbing impossible at exactly the moment the game gets interesting. */
  :global(#site-cat.boss) {
    z-index: 42;
  }
  :global(#site-cat.boss .cat-svg) {
    pointer-events: none;
    transform-origin: center bottom;
    transition: transform 0.12s ease-out;
  }
  /* wind-up: gather, ears back, hold. This is the 420ms you get to read. */
  :global(#site-cat.boss-telegraph .cat-svg) {
    transform: scale(1.06, 0.82);
  }
  :global(#site-cat.boss-telegraph .cat-ear) {
    transform: rotate(-24deg);
  }
  :global(#site-cat.boss-telegraph .cat-tail) {
    transform: rotate(-18deg);
  }
  /* The tell (§9.3). A real wind-up drops low and flattens its ears; a bluff gathers
     upright and keeps them half up. It is a small difference on purpose — §9.3 says the
     tell can be *learned*, which means it has to be missable the first few times. */
  :global(#site-cat.boss-feint .cat-svg) {
    transform: scale(1.02, 0.94);
  }
  :global(#site-cat.boss-feint .cat-ear) {
    transform: rotate(-9deg);
  }
  :global(#site-cat.boss-feint .cat-tail) {
    transform: rotate(-4deg);
  }

  /* in the air: stretched along the direction of travel */
  :global(#site-cat.boss-leap .cat-svg) {
    transform: scale(1.14, 1.06);
    transition: none;
  }
  /* landed on a paragraph, briefly regretting it */
  :global(#site-cat.boss-recover .cat-svg) {
    transform: scale(1.04, 0.9) rotate(-4deg);
  }
  :global(#site-cat.boss-recover .cat-tail) {
    transform: rotate(12deg);
  }
  /* head down in something better than you */
  :global(#site-cat.boss-eat .cat-svg) {
    transform: scale(1.02, 0.95);
  }
  :global(#site-cat.boss-eat .cat-head) {
    transform: translateY(3.5px);
  }
  :global(#site-cat.boss-eat .cat-tail) {
    transform: rotate(-6deg);
  }

  /* ---- the curtain (§14) ---- */
  #cat-curtain {
    position: fixed;
    inset: 0;
    /*
     * Explicit, and load-bearing. The backing store is sized in *grid* cells rather than
     * device pixels (see `runCurtain`), so the element has an intrinsic size of a few hundred
     * pixels — and an absolutely positioned replaced element with `width: auto` takes its
     * intrinsic width and drops the `right` offset on the floor. `inset: 0` alone would leave
     * a 256×180 curtain in the corner of the screen.
     */
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    z-index: 60;
    /* Never the thing under the cursor: a click aimed at the page mid-transition should
       be swallowed, not delivered to a page that is halfway through being claimed. */
    pointer-events: none;
    /*
     * The colour the screen floods toward, and it **darkens in both themes** (§14.4). In
     * light mode that is the ink. In dark mode the ink is a pale pink, and a full-screen
     * pale sheet over a dark page is a flashbang — so dark mode floods toward a ground
     * deepened almost to black instead. One luminance reversal, downward, either way.
     */
    --curtain-ink: #2a241e;
  }
  :global(html.dark) #cat-curtain {
    --curtain-ink: #0b0908;
  }
  #cat-curtain[hidden] {
    display: none;
  }

  /* ---- territory, on the top edge ---- */
  #cat-territory {
    position: fixed;
    inset: 0 0 auto 0;
    height: 3px;
    z-index: 46;
    pointer-events: none;
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-ink) 14%, transparent);
  }
  #cat-territory[hidden] {
    display: none;
  }
  .territory-mine {
    display: block;
    height: 100%;
    width: 0;
    background: var(--color-accent);
    /* Wide enough to read as movement, short enough that a reclaim feels immediate. The
       transition is the only thing that makes a 3px bar noticeable at all. */
    transition: width 0.45s ease-out;
  }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .territory-mine {
      transition: none;
    }
  }

  /* ---- the ribbon ---- */
  #cat-ribbon {
    position: fixed;
    left: 0;
    top: 0;
    z-index: 43;
    max-width: 15rem;
    padding: 0.28rem 0.5rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-line);
    border-radius: var(--radius-chip);
    background: var(--color-ground);
    color: var(--color-ink);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    line-height: 1.35;
    text-wrap: pretty;
    pointer-events: none;
    opacity: 0;
    transition: opacity 0.2s ease-out;
  }
  #cat-ribbon[hidden] {
    display: none;
  }
  #cat-ribbon.show {
    opacity: 0.96;
  }

  /* ---- a thrown treat ----
     Positioned exactly like the cat is: pinned to the bottom-left corner, moved by
     transform only, so one helper places both. */
  #cat-throw {
    position: fixed;
    left: 0;
    bottom: 0;
    z-index: 41;
    width: 20px;
    height: 20px;
    margin: -10px 0 0 -10px;
    pointer-events: none;
    color: var(--color-accent);
    opacity: 0.9;
  }
  #cat-throw[hidden] {
    display: none;
  }
  #cat-throw svg {
    display: block;
  }
  /* The cat has its head down on it — a small settle so the treat reads as being
     eaten rather than as having vanished. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    #cat-throw.eaten {
      animation: cat-treat-gone 0.4s ease-in forwards;
    }
    @keyframes cat-treat-gone {
      to {
        opacity: 0;
        transform: var(--throw-at) scale(0.4);
      }
    }
  }

  #cat-scrub {
    position: fixed;
    left: 0;
    top: 0;
    z-index: 41;
    width: 40px;
    height: 40px;
    margin: -20px 0 0 -20px;
    pointer-events: none;
    color: var(--color-accent);
    will-change: transform;
  }
  #cat-scrub svg {
    display: block;
    overflow: visible;
  }
  .scrub-track {
    stroke: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-ink) 22%, transparent);
  }
  .scrub-fill {
    stroke: currentColor;
    /* 2πr for r=17, set here so the script only ever writes the offset */
    stroke-dasharray: 106.81;
    stroke-dashoffset: 106.81;
  }
</style>

<script>
  import { motionReduced } from '@/lib/a11y-prefs';
  import { runCurtain, stop as stopCurtain, HOLD_FLOOR_MS, REVEAL_MS } from '@/lib/curtain';
  import { tallyFor, resolveTreat } from '@/lib/cat-game';
  import {
    CLAIMABLE,
    PROTECTED,
    PROTECTED_TREE,
    INTERACTIVE,
    INITIAL_CLAIM_FRACTION,
    HIDDEN_TRUCE_MS,
    IDLE_TRUCE_MS,
    AIM_LEAD_MS,
    LEAP_MS,
    OPENING_GRACE_MS,
    RECLAIM_ON_HIT,
    STALK_SPEED,
    THROW_ARC_MS,
    LURE_MS,
    FETCH_SPEED,
    FETCH_REACH,
    bigEnough,
    dropNested,
    boardSlice,
    pickClaims,
    STANCES,
    pickStance,
    treatSpec,
    type Stance,
    mood as moodFor,
    aggression,
    telegraphScale,
    patienceFor,
    talkGap,
    GROOM_EVERY_MS,
    GROOM_MS,
    type Mood,
    claimTilt,
    scrubProgress,
    stillEnough,
    arenaCaption,
    territory,
    isWon,
    isLost,
    LINE_MS,
    WIN_BEAT_MS,
    LOSE_BEAT_MS,
    pickLine,
    lineText,
    type FightState,
    nextPhase,
    canPounce,
    provoked,
    predict,
    leapPos,
    pounceHit,
    type Phase,
  } from '@/lib/arena';

  /**
   * Live state, module scope, session only.
   *
   * `on` is never read from anywhere but here, which is the asymmetry §13.4 was
   * reaching for: a page can only ever *load* with the arena off, so a visitor
   * can never arrive mid-invasion — not after a refresh, not on a return visit,
   * not from a shared link.
   */
  const arena = {
    on: false,
    /** Elements the cat currently holds, in DOM order. */
    claimed: [] as HTMLElement[],
    /** Every element it ever held, so a landed pounce has something to take back. */
    freed: [] as HTMLElement[],
    /** The board as queried, so a re-claim can find an element's original index. */
    order: [] as HTMLElement[],
    /** How many it started with, so the HUD line has a denominator. */
    total: 0,
    /** This fight's composition seed — re-claims must not re-roll the tilt. */
    seed: 0,
    /** When the fight began, for the opening grace (§7.4). */
    openedAt: 0,
    /**
     * The one treat allowed on the board, in flight or waiting to be eaten.
     *
     * One at a time is what keeps lures from being banked, now that a badly timed
     * throw waits to be useful instead of evaporating.
     */
    treat: null as null | {
      x: number;
      y: number;
      fx: number;
      fy: number;
      at: number;
      landed: boolean;
      /** Which shape, and so which effects — §9.5's loadout. */
      kind: string;
    },
    /** Which paws were filled when the fight opened, so spending is fight-local. */
    ammoWas: [] as boolean[],
    /** What the borrowed HUD line should say — see `holdCaption`. */
    caption: '',
    /** Treats thrown this fight, which the cat has opinions about (§8.4). */
    spent: 0,
    /** Consecutive interrupted scrubs, reset by a completed one. */
    interrupts: 0,
    /** When territory last changed, for the "nothing is happening" lines. */
    movedAt: 0,
    /** The line currently up, and when it went up. */
    lastLine: '' as string,
    spokeAt: 0,
    lineTimer: 0,
    /** Set once an ending has started, so nothing can end twice. */
    ending: null as null | 'win' | 'lose',
    /**
     * What the visitor has *asked* for, which is not the same as what is running.
     *
     * Step 6 opened a window — the length of the flood — in which the fight is committed to
     * but not yet on. Everything used to branch on `on`, so a second press inside that window
     * read as "not in a fight, start one": it aborted the curtain, opened the arena through
     * the abort path, and then opened it *again* when the second curtain reached its hold.
     * Two `open()`s with no `close()` between them replace `arena.claimed` wholesale and the
     * first board's claims are stranded on the page. Six presses at six beats left 12.
     *
     * So intent is what the toggle and Esc branch on, and it is set the instant the press
     * lands. This is also the better answer for §13.5: while a curtain is up, what the button
     * should say is what you asked it for.
     */
    want: false,
    /** Reverses the running curtain from wherever it has got to (§14.6). */
    curtain: null as null | (() => void),
    /** rAF handle for the loop. */
    raf: 0,
  };

  /** Scrub tracking: where the pointer settled, when, and how fast it is moving. */
  const scrub = {
    x: -1,
    y: -1,
    /** Where the current hold began. */
    ax: -1,
    ay: -1,
    /** Pointer velocity, px/s, for the cat to aim ahead of (§5.3). */
    vx: 0,
    vy: 0,
    at: 0,
    since: 0,
    target: null as HTMLElement | null,
    seen: 0,
    /**
     * Is the pointer even in the window?
     *
     * §5.3: a cursor that leaves mid-leap must be a whiff. Without this the last
     * known position lingers exactly where the cat aimed, so leaving the window
     * counted as standing still and being caught — the opposite of the rule.
     */
    inside: false,
  };

  const el = (id: string) => document.getElementById(id);

  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------- *
   * Building the board (§4.1)
   * ---------------------------------------------------------------- */

  /**
   * Everything on this page the cat could take.
   *
   * Queried, filtered, then de-nested — in that order, because `dropNested` is
   * O(n²) and the filters cut the list by about two thirds first.
   */
  function candidates(): HTMLElement[] {
    const all = [...document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(CLAIMABLE)];
    const keep = all.filter((node) => {
      if (node.matches(PROTECTED)) return false; // self only — see arena.ts
      if (node.closest(PROTECTED_TREE)) return false; // subtree
      const r = node.getBoundingClientRect();
      return bigEnough(r.width, r.height);
    });
    const nested = dropNested(keep, (outer, inner) => outer.contains(inner));
    // §4: the arena is the viewport plus what it can see. `boardSlice` keeps the fight on
    // the screen it is named after, and borrows from below the fold only if the screen is
    // too thin to hold a game.
    const vh = document.documentElement.clientHeight;
    const inView = nested.map((n) => {
      const r = n.getBoundingClientRect();
      return r.bottom > 0 && r.top < vh;
    });
    return boardSlice(inView).map((i) => nested[i]!);
  }

  /**
   * The `style` attribute each claimed element arrived with, verbatim.
   *
   * Undoing a claim property-by-property is not the same as putting the attribute
   * back: touching an element's style through the CSSOM re-serialises whatever was
   * already inline, so an element that came in with
   * `style="background-image:var(--lqip)"` can come out spelled differently and be
   * *visually* identical while no longer being the page as found. Pillar 2 says the
   * DOM is restored exactly, so hold the string and hand it back.
   */
  const wasStyled = new WeakMap<HTMLElement, string | null>();

  /** Paint a claim on. One custom property; the rest of the look is the class. */
  function claim(node: HTMLElement, index: number, seed: number): void {
    wasStyled.set(node, node.getAttribute('style'));
    node.style.setProperty('--claim-tilt', `${claimTilt(index, seed).toFixed(3)}deg`);
    node.classList.add('cat-claimed');
  }

  /** Take one back, leaving the element exactly as it was found. */
  function free(node: HTMLElement, celebrate: boolean): void {
    node.classList.remove('cat-claimed');
    const before = wasStyled.get(node) ?? null;
    if (before === null) node.removeAttribute('style');
    else node.setAttribute('style', before);
    wasStyled.delete(node);
    if (!celebrate) return;
    node.classList.add('cat-freed');
    setTimeout(() => node.classList.remove('cat-freed'), 480);
  }

  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------- *
   * The HUD line — borrowed, then given back
   * ---------------------------------------------------------------- */

  function caption(text: string): void {
    arena.caption = text;
    const line = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('#cat-score .cat-caption');
    if (line && line.textContent !== text) line.textContent = text;
  }

  /**
   * Hold the line the arena borrowed.
   *
   * SiteCat's `announce()` swaps the caption to the level phrase on a find and sets a
   * 3.2s timer to put the tally back. That timer does not know about the fight, so it
   * fired mid-fight and left the caption reading "3 / 7 treats" while the paw row
   * beside it was showing 2 — the row is ammo during a fight, so the two were flatly
   * contradicting each other. Caught in a screenshot, not by a test.
   *
   * Re-asserting once a frame is the cheap fix and it is self-healing: whatever writes
   * to that line, it goes back to the truth on the next tick.
   */
  function holdCaption(): void {
    if (!arena.caption) return;
    caption(arena.caption);
  }

  /**
   * Put the treat tally back exactly as the cat had it.
   *
   * Recomputed rather than remembered: SiteCat owns that state and may well have
   * changed it while the fight was on (arriving on a new tab credits a treat), so
   * a string cached at the start would be stale. The paw row is the source of
   * truth for both numbers, which is the same trick `readTabs` uses.
   */
  function restoreCaption(): void {
    const paws = [...document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('#cat-score .cat-paw[data-slug]')];
    if (!paws.length) return;
    caption(tallyFor(paws.filter((p) => p.classList.contains('got')).length, paws.length));
  }

  /**
   * Give the paw row back exactly as it was found.
   *
   * §7.2: losing a fight must never cost you exploration, so treats spent here are
   * spent *inside* the fight only. Must run before `restoreCaption`, which counts the
   * filled paws to rebuild the tally — restoring in the other order publishes the
   * ammo count as if it were the score.
   */
  function restoreAmmo(): void {
    const paws = [...document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('#cat-score .cat-paw[data-slug]')];
    paws.forEach((paw, i) => paw.classList.toggle('got', arena.ammoWas[i] ?? paw.classList.contains('got')));
    arena.ammoWas = [];
  }

  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------- *
   * Scrub loop
   * ---------------------------------------------------------------- */

  const ring = () => el('cat-scrub');

  function showRing(x: number, y: number, progress: number): void {
    const r = ring();
    if (!r) return;
    r.hidden = false;
    r.style.transform = `translate(${x}px, ${y}px)`;
    const fill = r.querySelector<SVGCircleElement>('.scrub-fill');
    // 106.81 = 2πr, matching the dasharray in the stylesheet
    fill?.style.setProperty('stroke-dashoffset', String(106.81 * (1 - progress)));
  }

  function hideRing(): void {
    const r = ring();
    if (r) r.hidden = true;
  }

  /** The claimed element under the pointer, if any. */
  function claimUnder(x: number, y: number): HTMLElement | null {
    const hit = document.elementFromPoint(x, y);
    if (!(hit instanceof Element)) return null;
    const node = hit.closest<HTMLElement>('.cat-claimed');
    return node && arena.claimed.includes(node) ? node : null;
  }

  /**
   * One frame of scrubbing. Returns progress 0..1, which the boss reads to decide
   * whether you are committed enough to be worth interrupting.
   */
  function stepScrub(now: number): number {
    const target = scrub.inside && scrub.x >= 0 ? claimUnder(scrub.x, scrub.y) : null;
    if (!target || target !== scrub.target) {
      // moved onto something else, or off the board: the hold restarts
      scrub.target = target;
      scrub.since = now;
      scrub.ax = scrub.x;
      scrub.ay = scrub.y;
      if (!target) hideRing();
      return 0;
    }
    if (!stillEnough(scrub.x - scrub.ax, scrub.y - scrub.ay)) {
      // drifted: same target, fresh hold, and the anchor follows the pointer
      scrub.since = now;
      scrub.ax = scrub.x;
      scrub.ay = scrub.y;
    }
    const progress = scrubProgress(now - scrub.since);
    showRing(scrub.x, scrub.y, progress);
    if (progress < 1) return progress;

    // reclaimed
    arena.claimed = arena.claimed.filter((n) => n !== target);
    arena.freed.push(target);
    arena.interrupts = 0;
    arena.movedAt = now;
    free(target, true);
    caption(arenaCaption(arena.claimed.length, arena.total));
    paintTerritory();
    scrub.target = null;
    scrub.since = now;
    hideRing();
    if (isWon(arena.claimed.length, arena.order.length)) finish('win', now);
    return 0;
  }

  function tick(now: number): void {
    if (!arena.on) return;
    arena.raf = requestAnimationFrame(tick);

    // auto-truce: a fight nobody is playing is over (§11)
    if (scrub.seen && now - scrub.seen > IDLE_TRUCE_MS) {
      endQuietly();
      return;
    }

    // Clamped, like the ink wash clamps its own: a tab that comes back from the
    // background hands you a multi-second frame, and an unclamped stalk would
    // teleport the cat onto the cursor and pounce from nowhere.
    const dt = Math.min(0.05, (now - boss.last) / 1000);
    boss.last = now;

    // Treat first: a landing has to be visible to the boss in the same frame, or the
    // cat spends a frame stalking something it should already have abandoned.
    stepTreat(now);
    // Then the mood, before anything reads it: the tier decides how fast the cat winds
    // up, how soon it commits, how quickly it walks and how often it speaks, so a
    // stale one for a frame means four things disagreeing about the same fight.
    stepMood(now);
    stepBoss(now, dt, stepScrub(now));
    holdCaption();
    // Nothing talks while an ending is playing: the ending has the last word.
    if (!arena.ending) stepTalk(now);
  }

  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------- *
   * The boss (§5.3) — stalk, telegraph, leap, recover
   * ---------------------------------------------------------------- */

  /** The cat, in client coordinates, as a thing with one phase at a time. */
  const boss = {
    x: 0,
    y: 0,
    phase: 'stalk' as Phase,
    /** When the current phase began. */
    since: 0,
    /** Leap endpoints, locked when the telegraph ends. */
    ax: 0,
    ay: 0,
    bx: 0,
    by: 0,
    facing: 1 as 1 | -1,
    last: 0,
    /** This fight's stance (§9.3) — same verbs, different opponent. */
    stance: 'ambush' as Stance,
    /** True when the current wind-up is a bluff (trickster). */
    feint: false,
    /** A bell makes the *next* telegraph readable; consumed when it fires. */
    telegraphMul: 1,
    /** Yarn tangles its feet: slowed until this timestamp. */
    slowUntil: 0,
    slowMul: 1,
    /** Feather and biscuit pin it near a spot: it will not stray past this. */
    anchorX: 0,
    anchorY: 0,
    anchorPx: 0,
    anchorUntil: 0,
    /** Siege regrows the board on this clock. */
    regrowAt: 0,
    /** How long the cat cannot pounce while at a treat, from the treat's spec. */
    immuneMs: 0,
    lureMs: 0,
    /**
     * How the fight is going, as the cat reads it (§7.3).
     *
     * Held rather than recomputed from scratch each frame because `mood` is hysteretic:
     * which tier you are in depends on which tier you were in, and a boundary with no
     * memory strobes on alternate exchanges.
     */
    mood: 'even' as Mood,
    /** When the bored cat last washed, and until when it is busy doing so. */
    groomedAt: 0,
    groomUntil: 0,
  };

  const catEl = () => el('site-cat');

  /** Half the cat, measured rather than hardcoded — SiteCat owns its own size. */
  function catHalf(root: HTMLElement): { w: number; h: number } {
    return { w: root.offsetWidth / 2, h: root.offsetHeight / 2 };
  }

  /**
   * Put the cat's centre at a client point.
   *
   * `#site-cat` is pinned `left: 0; bottom: 0`, so its untransformed centre sits at
   * (halfW, viewportH - halfH) and everything is an offset from there. Writing the
   * transform is the only thing this component does to the element itself.
   */
  function placeCat(root: HTMLElement, cx: number, cy: number): void {
    const { w, h } = catHalf(root);
    const vw = document.documentElement.clientWidth;
    const vh = document.documentElement.clientHeight;
    const x = Math.max(w, Math.min(vw - w, cx));
    const y = Math.max(h, Math.min(vh - h, cy));
    boss.x = x;
    boss.y = y;
    root.style.transform = `translate(${(x - w).toFixed(1)}px, ${(y - vh + h).toFixed(1)}px)`;
  }

  function bossPhase(root: HTMLElement, phase: Phase, now: number): void {
    boss.phase = phase;
    boss.since = now;
    root.classList.toggle('boss-telegraph', phase === 'telegraph');
    root.classList.toggle('boss-leap', phase === 'leap');
    root.classList.toggle('boss-recover', phase === 'recover');
    root.classList.toggle('boss-eat', phase === 'eat');
    root.classList.toggle('boss-fetch', phase === 'fetch');
    // §9.3 says the trickster's feint can be learned, which means there has to be a tell.
    // This is it: a bluff gathers without dropping its shoulders.
    root.classList.toggle('boss-feint', phase === 'telegraph' && boss.feint);
    /*
     * `walking` is SiteCat's own class, and its leg and bob keyframes are already
     * written. The boss was moving with its legs still: the arena parks SiteCat's
     * loop, and that loop is what used to set this. Reusing it means the two phases
     * that actually travel animate, and nothing new is drawn.
     */
    root.classList.toggle('walking', phase === 'stalk' || phase === 'fetch');
  }

  /**
   * Stop washing, whatever the reason.
   *
   * Its own function because it is called from four places — the wash finishing, the mood
   * lifting mid-wash, handing the cat back, and closing the fight — and because the class
   * belongs to `SiteCat`. Leaving it set would hand the ambient cat a grooming class it
   * never asked for; `idleBusiness()` would eventually clear it, but only once the cat
   * next goes idle, which is not a guarantee worth relying on for somebody else's element.
   */
  function endGrooming(root: HTMLElement | null): void {
    boss.groomUntil = 0;
    root?.classList.remove('grooming');
  }

  /** Turn to face the way it is going. `.face-left` is SiteCat's own class. */
  function faceToward(root: HTMLElement, dx: number): void {
    const face = dx < 0 ? -1 : 1;
    if (face === boss.facing) return;
    boss.facing = face as 1 | -1;
    root.classList.toggle('face-left', face === -1);
  }

  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------- *
   * Treats (§5.4) — exploration, spent
   * ---------------------------------------------------------------- */

  const throwEl = () => el('cat-throw');

  /**
   * Ammunition: the filled paws in the HUD row.
   *
   * Pillar 5 — the fight is a *sink* for the treat economy the site already has, not
   * a new one. So a visitor who has browsed nothing has nothing to throw, and the tab
   * bar is the tutorial. §5.4 calls that state normal rather than an error: the cat
   * just looks at you, and silence is the feedback.
   */
  function ammo(): HTMLElement[] {
    return [...document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('#cat-score .cat-paw.got')];
  }

  /** Put the treat somewhere, in the same coordinates the cat uses. */
  function placeThrow(node: HTMLElement, x: number, y: number): void {
    const vh = document.documentElement.clientHeight;
    const t = `translate(${x.toFixed(1)}px, ${(y - vh).toFixed(1)}px)`;
    // the custom property lets the "being eaten" keyframe scale *about* this spot
    // instead of throwing the treat back to the corner to shrink
    node.style.setProperty('--throw-at', t);
    node.style.transform = t;
  }

  /**
   * Spend one treat at the pointer.
   *
   * Three ways this does nothing, all silent and all costing nothing: no ammo, a treat
   * already on the board, or the arena not running. §5.4 asks for silence on the first
   * — an error state for touching a game you opted into would be absurd — and the
   * second is what stops lures being banked now that a badly timed throw is no longer
   * "wasted" (see the note in the GDD's 0.5 entry).
   */
  function throwTreat(x: number, y: number): void {
    if (!arena.on || arena.treat) return;
    const paws = ammo();
    const paw = paws[paws.length - 1];
    if (!paw) return;
    const node = throwEl();
    if (!node) return;

    // The shape is the shape of the treat being spent — the behaviour is one type for
    // every throw (step 3 is "a single type"), but the row shows what you used, which
    // is what §9.5's loadout will eventually make matter.
    const kind = resolveTreat(paw.dataset.slug ?? '');
    node.querySelector('use')?.setAttribute('href', `#treat-${kind}`);
    paw.classList.remove('got');

    // thrown from the stash, which is the widget the paws live in
    const hud = el('cat-hud')?.getBoundingClientRect();
    const from = hud
      ? { x: hud.left + hud.width / 2, y: hud.top + hud.height / 2 }
      : { x: 20, y: document.documentElement.clientHeight - 20 };
    arena.treat = { x, y, fx: from.x, fy: from.y, at: performance.now(), landed: false, kind };
    arena.spent++;
    node.classList.remove('eaten');
    node.removeAttribute('hidden');
    placeThrow(node, from.x, from.y);
  }

  /** One frame of a treat in the air. */
  function stepTreat(now: number): void {
    const t = arena.treat;
    const node = throwEl();
    if (!t || !node || t.landed) return;
    const k = (now - t.at) / THROW_ARC_MS;
    if (k >= 1) {
      t.landed = true;
      placeThrow(node, t.x, t.y);
      return;
    }
    // reuse the leap's arc: same hop, thrown rather than jumped
    const p = leapPos(t.fx, t.fy, t.x, t.y, k);
    placeThrow(node, p.x, p.y);
  }

  /** The cat has finished with it. */
  function finishEating(root: HTMLElement, now: number): void {
    const node = throwEl();
    node?.setAttribute('hidden', '');
    node?.classList.remove('eaten');
    arena.treat = null;
    bossPhase(root, 'stalk', now);
  }

  /** Wipe any treat off the board without the cat having eaten it (truce). */
  function clearTreat(): void {
    const node = throwEl();
    node?.setAttribute('hidden', '');
    node?.classList.remove('eaten');
    arena.treat = null;
  }

  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------- *
   * Territory (§6) and dialogue (§8)
   * ---------------------------------------------------------------- */

  /** Paint the bar. Player's share, so it grows as you win. */
  function paintTerritory(arrive = false): void {
    const bar = el('cat-territory');
    if (!bar) return;
    const mine = 1 - territory(arena.claimed.length, arena.order.length);
    bar.hidden = false;
    const fill = bar.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.territory-mine');
    if (!fill) return;
    const width = `${(mine * 100).toFixed(1)}%`;
    if (!arrive) {
      fill.style.transitionDelay = '';
      fill.style.width = width;
      return;
    }
    /*
     * §14.3 wants the bar to *arrive* rather than to be found already there, and to arrive
     * from an edge, because direction is meaning. A 3px strip cannot slide anywhere legibly,
     * so what arrives is the **fill**, measured out from the left while the ink dries off it.
     *
     * Two problems, both about timing rather than looks. A width transition on an element
     * that was `display: none` one statement ago has nothing to transition *from*, hence the
     * double rAF. And `open()` runs during the hold, with the screen opaque — so a sweep
     * starting immediately would happen entirely behind the curtain.
     *
     * The delay is `HOLD_FLOOR_MS + 55% of the reveal`, not merely the hold, and the extra is
     * measured rather than taste: drying is a smoothstep, so the sheet is still at 0.99 cover
     * a fifth of the way into the reveal and only opens up past the halfway mark. Waiting for
     * the hold alone put one growth frame in six where it could be seen.
     */
    fill.style.width = '0%';
    fill.style.transitionDelay = `${Math.round(HOLD_FLOOR_MS + REVEAL_MS * 0.55)}ms`;
    requestAnimationFrame(() => {
      requestAnimationFrame(() => {
        fill.style.width = width;
      });
    });
  }

  function hideTerritory(): void {
    const bar = el('cat-territory');
    if (!bar) return;
    bar.hidden = true;
    // Attribute off rather than width zeroed: these elements are persisted across
    // navigation, so a stale inline style would ride along for the rest of the visit.
    bar.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.territory-mine')?.removeAttribute('style');
  }

  /**
   * Say something, for `LINE_MS`, anchored above the cat.
   *
   * Not tracked per frame: the ribbon is placed once, where the cat was when it spoke.
   * A label that follows a leaping cat around is a label nobody can read, and the line
   * outliving the pounce it belongs to is the point.
   */
  function say(id: string, ms = LINE_MS): void {
    const node = el('cat-ribbon');
    if (!node) return;
    arena.lastLine = id;
    arena.spokeAt = performance.now();
    node.textContent = lineText(id);
    node.hidden = false;
    // measure after the text is in, or the first line of a fight is placed as if empty
    const cat = catEl()?.getBoundingClientRect();
    const w = node.offsetWidth;
    const x = cat ? cat.left + cat.width / 2 - w / 2 : 24;
    const y = cat ? cat.top - node.offsetHeight - 8 : 24;
    node.style.transform = `translate(${Math.max(8, Math.min(document.documentElement.clientWidth - w - 8, x)).toFixed(0)}px, ${Math.max(8, y).toFixed(0)}px)`;
    requestAnimationFrame(() => node.classList.add('show'));
    clearTimeout(arena.lineTimer);
    arena.lineTimer = window.setTimeout(() => {
      node.classList.remove('show');
      setTimeout(() => {
        if (!node.classList.contains('show')) node.hidden = true;
      }, 220);
    }, ms);
  }

  function hushRibbon(): void {
    const node = el('cat-ribbon');
    clearTimeout(arena.lineTimer);
    if (!node) return;
    node.classList.remove('show');
    node.hidden = true;
    node.removeAttribute('style');
    node.textContent = '';
  }

  /** Gather what the cat is reacting to. */
  function fightState(now: number, ending?: FightState['ending']): FightState {
    return {
      territory: territory(arena.claimed.length, arena.order.length),
      ammo: ammo().length,
      spent: arena.spent,
      freed: arena.freed.length,
      interrupts: arena.interrupts,
      idleMs: scrub.seen ? now - scrub.seen : 0,
      staleMs: now - arena.movedAt,
      collared: !!catEl()?.classList.contains('lv6'),
      mood: boss.mood,
      ending,
    };
  }

  /**
   * Re-read how the fight is going (§7.3).
   *
   * Fed its own previous answer, which is the hysteresis rather than a shortcut — see
   * `mood` in `arena.ts`. Called once a frame, before anything consumes the tier, so the
   * boss and the ribbon are never a frame apart about what kind of fight this is.
   */
  function stepMood(now: number): void {
    const next = moodFor(fightState(now), boss.mood);
    if (next === boss.mood) return;
    boss.mood = next;
    // Published the same way and for the same reasons as `data-stance`: the tier is a real
    // piece of fight state, it is worth being able to see from outside the closure, and a
    // stylesheet may eventually want it. Written only on change — this runs every frame.
    const root = catEl();
    if (root) root.dataset.mood = next;
  }

  /** One frame of deciding whether to speak. */
  function stepTalk(now: number): void {
    // §7.3's "more taunting": the gap between lines is the mood's, not a constant. A
    // desperate cat crowds you; a bored one goes quiet as well as still.
    if (now - arena.spokeAt < LINE_MS + talkGap(aggression(boss.mood))) return;
    const id = pickLine(fightState(now), arena.lastLine);
    if (id) say(id);
  }

  function stepBoss(now: number, dt: number, progress: number): void {
    const root = catEl();
    if (!root) return;

    // Stances bend durations, they do not replace them (§9.3). A bell's doubled telegraph
    // rides on top of the stance's own multiplier and is spent when the wind-up ends.
    const spec = STANCES[boss.stance];
    const aggro = aggression(boss.mood);
    const scale =
      boss.phase === 'telegraph'
        ? // §7.3's desperate tier, and it only ever shortens this — see `telegraphScale`.
          // Recovery is deliberately absent from this list: scaling it too breaks §10's
          // whiff invariant for siege, and a dodge has to stay worth making precisely
          // when the cat is desperate.
          spec.telegraph * boss.telegraphMul * telegraphScale(aggro)
        : boss.phase === 'recover'
          ? spec.recover
          : boss.phase === 'eat'
            ? boss.lureMs / LURE_MS
            : 1;
    const advance = nextPhase(boss.phase, now - boss.since, scale);
    if (advance === 'leap') {
      // The target was locked when the telegraph began (see `aim` below) — nothing
      // is decided here. That is the whole point: the wind-up is a commitment, so
      // the 420ms of it is a window you can actually dodge in.
      boss.telegraphMul = 1; // the bell's gift is spent on the wind-up it read
      if (boss.feint) {
        // A trickster's bluff: it gathers, thinks better of it, and goes back to stalking.
        // No leap, no recovery — the cost of a feint is the time, which is what makes
        // spending a treat on one feel like being had.
        boss.feint = false;
        bossPhase(root, 'stalk', now);
        return;
      }
      bossPhase(root, 'leap', now);
    } else if (advance === 'recover') {
      bossPhase(root, 'recover', now);
      land(root, now);
    } else if (advance === 'stalk' && boss.phase === 'eat') {
      finishEating(root, now);
    } else if (advance) {
      bossPhase(root, advance, now);
    }

    if (boss.phase === 'leap') {
      const t = (now - boss.since) / LEAP_MS;
      const p = leapPos(boss.ax, boss.ay, boss.bx, boss.by, t);
      placeCat(root, p.x, p.y);
      return;
    }

    // Going to get it. Faster than stalking, and — the whole value of the resource —
    // it cannot pounce from here, so this is the window the throw bought.
    if (boss.phase === 'fetch') {
      const t = arena.treat;
      if (!t) {
        bossPhase(root, 'stalk', now);
        return;
      }
      const tx = t.x - boss.x;
      const ty = t.y - boss.y;
      const away = Math.hypot(tx, ty);
      if (away <= FETCH_REACH) {
        /*
         * §9.5, applied at the moment it starts eating. Every effect is a number on the
         * boss, never a new mechanic: how long it is occupied, how long of that it cannot
         * pounce, whether it is pinned near the spot afterwards, and two modifiers it
         * carries away with it.
         */
        const spec = treatSpec(t.kind);
        boss.lureMs = spec.lureMs;
        boss.immuneMs = spec.immuneMs;
        boss.anchorX = t.x;
        boss.anchorY = t.y;
        boss.anchorPx = spec.anchorPx;
        boss.anchorUntil = spec.anchorMs ? now + spec.lureMs + spec.anchorMs : 0;
        boss.telegraphMul = spec.telegraphMul;
        boss.slowMul = spec.slowMul;
        boss.slowUntil = spec.slowMs ? now + spec.lureMs + spec.slowMs : 0;
        throwEl()?.classList.add('eaten');
        bossPhase(root, 'eat', now);
        return;
      }
      const step = Math.min(away, FETCH_SPEED * dt);
      placeCat(root, boss.x + (tx / away) * step, boss.y + (ty / away) * step);
      faceToward(root, tx);
      return;
    }

    /*
     * Eating with the immunity already spent (§9.5's biscuit): it is still at the treat, but
     * it can pounce again. Handled by leaving the `eat` phase early while the anchor keeps
     * it in place — one mechanism, two treats, and the reason "shortest immunity but stays
     * put longest" is not the contradiction it reads as.
     */
    if (boss.phase === 'eat' && boss.immuneMs < boss.lureMs && now - boss.since >= boss.immuneMs) {
      finishEating(root, now);
    }

    // telegraph, recover and eat all hold still — a crouch, a faceplant, and a cat
    // with its head down in something better than you
    if (boss.phase !== 'stalk') return;

    // A landed treat outranks the cursor: food first, which is exactly what makes the
    // throw a decision rather than a distraction.
    if (arena.treat?.landed) {
      bossPhase(root, 'fetch', now);
      return;
    }

    /*
     * §7.3's bored tell: the cat stops chasing you and washes.
     *
     * A pause, not just an animation. "Gets bored, grooms itself" is only legible if the
     * cat visibly gives up on you for a moment — a head-wash performed while still
     * marching at your cursor reads as a glitch, not as indifference. So this holds it
     * still and takes the pounce away for the duration, which is also the mercy the tier
     * exists to deliver.
     *
     * `.grooming` and its keyframe are SiteCat's, already written for affection tier 3
     * (§0: nothing new is drawn). Re-added through a reflow because a non-looping CSS
     * animation will not replay for a class that is already present — the same trick the
     * arena note's nudge uses.
     */
    if (boss.mood === 'bored' && now >= boss.groomUntil && now - boss.groomedAt > GROOM_EVERY_MS) {
      boss.groomedAt = now;
      boss.groomUntil = now + GROOM_MS;
      root.classList.remove('grooming');
      void root.offsetWidth;
      root.classList.add('grooming');
    }
    if (now < boss.groomUntil) {
      // Busy. Not stalking, not pouncing.
      if (boss.mood !== 'bored') endGrooming(root);
      else return;
    } else {
      endGrooming(root);
    }

    if (!scrub.inside || scrub.x < 0) return; // nothing to stalk
    const dx = scrub.x - boss.x;
    const dy = scrub.y - boss.y;
    const dist = Math.hypot(dx, dy);

    if (
      canPounce(boss.phase) &&
      now - arena.openedAt > OPENING_GRACE_MS &&
      // The stance's patience and the mood's, composed and clamped (§7.3): a bored cat
      // waits until you have nearly finished before it can be bothered, a desperate one
      // commits as soon as you have started.
      provoked(dist, progress, patienceFor(spec.patience, aggro)) &&
      arena.claimed.length > 0
    ) {
      /*
       * Commit. Everything about the pounce is decided here and nothing after.
       *
       * Velocity is only meaningful while the pointer is moving: a cursor that
       * stopped keeps its last sampled reading, because nothing fires to say "still
       * now", and aiming ahead of it would make the cat miss the one player it is
       * certainly entitled to hit — the one holding perfectly still.
       */
      const moving = now - scrub.at < 90;
      const aim = predict(
        scrub.x,
        scrub.y,
        moving ? scrub.vx : 0,
        moving ? scrub.vy : 0,
        AIM_LEAD_MS,
      );
      boss.ax = boss.x;
      boss.ay = boss.y;
      boss.bx = aim.x;
      boss.by = aim.y;
      boss.feint = Math.random() < spec.feint;
      bossPhase(root, 'telegraph', now);
      return;
    }

    if (dist > 1) {
      const slowed = now < boss.slowUntil ? boss.slowMul : 1;
      // Mood is the fourth multiplier here, after the stance, the yarn and the frame: a
      // bored cat ambles, a desperate one hurries. Unlike the telegraph this one scales
      // both ways — walking slowly costs the player nothing, so there is no invariant to
      // protect, and an ambling cat is the most legible tell the bored tier has after
      // the grooming.
      const step = Math.min(dist, STALK_SPEED * spec.stalk * slowed * aggro * dt);
      let nx = boss.x + (dx / dist) * step;
      let ny = boss.y + (dy / dist) * step;
      // Anchored by a feather (or the tail of a biscuit): it will come this far and no
      // further, which is what makes a thrown treat a *place* rather than a delay.
      if (now < boss.anchorUntil && boss.anchorPx > 0) {
        const ax = nx - boss.anchorX;
        const ay = ny - boss.anchorY;
        const away = Math.hypot(ax, ay);
        if (away > boss.anchorPx) {
          nx = boss.anchorX + (ax / away) * boss.anchorPx;
          ny = boss.anchorY + (ay / away) * boss.anchorPx;
        }
      }
      // Siege never leaves the floor: it moves in x only, so it is barely a threat and the
      // board has to do its fighting for it.
      if (spec.pin) ny = boss.y;
      placeCat(root, nx, ny);
      faceToward(root, dx);
    }

    // Siege's other half: the page grows back.
    if (spec.regrowMs > 0 && now > boss.regrowAt && arena.claimed.length < arena.order.length) {
      boss.regrowAt = now + spec.regrowMs;
      const back = takeGround();
      if (back) {
        claim(back, arena.order.indexOf(back), arena.seed);
        arena.claimed.push(back);
        arena.total = Math.max(arena.total, arena.claimed.length);
        arena.movedAt = now;
        caption(arenaCaption(arena.claimed.length, arena.total));
        paintTerritory();
        if (isLost(arena.claimed.length, arena.order.length, ammo().length)) finish('lose', now);
      }
    }
  }

  /**
   * Touchdown. Hit or miss is decided against where the cat *aimed*.
   *
   * A hit costs you the hold and one element back; §7.2 still applies, so it costs
   * nothing outside this fight. A miss costs the cat `RECOVER_MS` plus the walk
   * back from wherever you were standing when it committed — which is the real
   * reward for dodging, and why the player choosing *where* to flee matters.
   */
  function land(root: HTMLElement, now: number): void {
    placeCat(root, boss.bx, boss.by);
    const caught = scrub.inside && pounceHit(boss.bx, boss.by, scrub.x, scrub.y);
    if (!caught) return;

    // the hold is gone, not reduced: partial retention would remove the reason to
    // ever dodge (§5.2)
    scrub.target = null;
    scrub.since = now;
    hideRing();
    arena.interrupts++;

    for (let i = 0; i < RECLAIM_ON_HIT; i++) {
      const back = takeGround();
      if (!back) break;
      claim(back, arena.order.indexOf(back), arena.seed);
      arena.claimed.push(back);
      arena.total = Math.max(arena.total, arena.claimed.length);
    }
    arena.movedAt = now;
    caption(arenaCaption(arena.claimed.length, arena.total));
    paintTerritory();
    if (isLost(arena.claimed.length, arena.order.length, ammo().length)) finish('lose', now);
  }

  /**
   * What a landed pounce takes.
   *
   * The most recently freed element first — taking back the thing you just earned is
   * legible as cause and effect in a way a random element is not. But **a loss has to be
   * reachable**, and until 0.6 this only ever took from the freed pile: a player who had
   * freed nothing could not fall below the opening 55%, so territory could never reach
   * 100% and §2's losing condition could not fire. Failing that, the cat takes the thing
   * it just landed on, which is the most legible claim available; failing *that*, the
   * nearest unclaimed thing.
   */
  function takeGround(): HTMLElement | null {
    while (arena.freed.length) {
      const back = arena.freed.pop();
      if (back?.isConnected) return back;
    }
    const free = arena.order.filter((n) => n.isConnected && !arena.claimed.includes(n));
    if (!free.length) return null;
    const under = document.elementFromPoint(boss.bx, boss.by);
    const landedOn = under instanceof Element ? free.find((n) => n === under || n.contains(under)) : null;
    if (landedOn) return landedOn;
    let best = free[0]!;
    let bestD = Infinity;
    for (const n of free) {
      const r = n.getBoundingClientRect();
      const d = Math.hypot(r.left + r.width / 2 - boss.bx, r.top + r.height / 2 - boss.by);
      if (d < bestD) {
        bestD = d;
        best = n;
      }
    }
    return best;
  }

  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------- *
   * Endings (§7, §8.4)
   * ---------------------------------------------------------------- */

  /**
   * End the fight with a beat rather than a cut.
   *
   * The win beat is §7.4's hook: the cat takes **one** element back and leaves, so the
   * fight ends on unfinished business instead of a fanfare. It is theatre and not a
   * state — the page is restored whole a second later, because a byte-identical restore
   * is pillar 2 and is not negotiable for the sake of a joke.
   */
  function finish(ending: 'win' | 'lose', now: number): void {
    if (arena.ending) return;
    arena.ending = ending;
    const line = pickLine(fightState(now, ending), undefined);
    if (line) say(line, ending === 'win' ? WIN_BEAT_MS : LOSE_BEAT_MS);

    if (ending === 'win') {
      // the notch, for the rest of the session (§7.1)
      catEl()?.classList.add('notched');
      const parting = takeGround();
      if (parting) {
        claim(parting, arena.order.indexOf(parting), arena.seed);
        arena.claimed.push(parting);
        paintTerritory();
      }
    }
    setTimeout(() => setArena(false), ending === 'win' ? WIN_BEAT_MS : LOSE_BEAT_MS);
  }

  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------- *
   * On / off — one code path, so the DOM can only be restored one way
   * ---------------------------------------------------------------- */

  /**
   * The only way in or out. Esc, the toggle, a cleared board and both auto-truces
   * all come through here, so there is exactly one implementation of "restore the
   * page" — a second one is a second thing that can leave a claim behind.
   *
   * Idempotent by the first line. §13.6's latch (a press arriving *during* the
   * transition) belongs with the transition, which is step 6; an instant swap has
   * no window to land in.
   */
  /**
   * The way in and out, behind the curtain (§14).
   *
   * The transition is decoration and the state change is not, so every path here ends with
   * `open()` or `close()` having run exactly once: no canvas, no 2D context, a device too
   * slow to finish inside `WALL_MS`, a tab hidden halfway, or the visitor pressing Esc
   * during the flood. That is why step 0 built the instant swap first — this is a layer on
   * top of a thing that already worked (§14.6).
   *
   * `arena.curtain` holds the reverse handle: an interruption that has to wait for an
   * animation to finish is not an interruption.
   */
  function setArena(on: boolean): void {
    if (on === arena.want) return;
    arena.want = on;
    // The button answers the press, not the animation.
    syncToggle();
    arena.curtain?.();
    arena.curtain = null;
    const canvas = el('cat-curtain') as HTMLCanvasElement | null;
    const swap = (behindCurtain: boolean) => {
      if (on) open(behindCurtain);
      else close();
      syncToggle();
    };
    if (!canvas || motionReduced()) {
      // §14.5: never a half-speed flood. Under reduced motion the arena does not open at
      // all today (§11), so this is really the no-canvas path.
      swap(false);
      return;
    }
    arena.curtain = runCurtain(canvas, on ? 'in' : 'out', () => swap(true));
  }

  /**
   * A truce the visitor asked for, which gets its own line (§8.4).
   *
   * Said *before* closing, because `say` anchors the ribbon to where the cat is and the
   * cat goes back to walking the moment the fight ends. The line then outlives the fight
   * by design: the page is already restored while the cat is still muttering about it,
   * which is the only place in the build where the ribbon is deliberately louder than
   * the game.
   */
  /**
   * End it with nothing said.
   *
   * The auto-truces, reduce-motion, and navigating away are not the visitor choosing to
   * stop, so the cat gets no parting shot — a line like "sensible." on a page you
   * *didn't* leave deliberately would be the cat taking credit for a timer. This is also
   * the only path that hushes the ribbon, since the deliberate ones are said on the way
   * out and have to outlive the fight.
   */
  function endQuietly(): void {
    hushRibbon();
    setArena(false);
  }

  function truce(): void {
    if (!arena.want) return;
    if (!arena.on) {
      // Esc during the flood. There is no fight yet to have an opinion about how it went,
      // so this is a cancelled commitment rather than a truce, and the cat says nothing.
      setArena(false);
      return;
    }
    if (arena.ending) {
      // an ending is already playing; let it have the last word
      setArena(false);
      return;
    }
    const kind = boss.phase === 'recover' ? 'truce-recover' : 'truce';
    const line = pickLine(fightState(performance.now(), kind), undefined);
    if (line) say(line, LINE_MS);
    setArena(false);
  }

  /**
   * `behindCurtain` is not cosmetic bookkeeping: it is the difference between the bar
   * sweeping into a reveal the visitor can see and sweeping behind an opaque sheet.
   */
  function open(behindCurtain = false): void {
    /*
     * A second open over a live fight would replace `arena.claimed` wholesale and leave the
     * first board claimed with nothing tracking it — and worse, `claim()` snapshots the
     * element's style into `wasStyled`, so claiming an already-claimed node records the
     * *tilted* style as the original and `free()` faithfully restores the tilt. Closing first
     * costs nothing on the normal path and makes that unreachable rather than unlikely.
     */
    if (arena.on) close();
    const board = candidates();
    if (!board.length) {
      // Nothing to fight over. The intent has to be given back too, or the button stays
      // pressed over an arena that never opened — §13.5's exact failure state.
      arena.want = false;
      return;
    }
    const seed = (Date.now() ^ (Math.random() * 0xffffffff)) >>> 0;
    const picked = pickClaims(board.length, INITIAL_CLAIM_FRACTION, seed);
    arena.order = board;
    arena.seed = seed;
    arena.claimed = picked.map((i) => board[i]!);
    arena.freed = [];
    arena.total = arena.claimed.length;
    // index by position in the *board*, not in the claimed subset, so a re-claim
    // after a landed pounce reproduces the tilt the element had the first time
    arena.claimed.forEach((node) => claim(node, board.indexOf(node), seed));
    arena.on = true;
    document.documentElement.classList.add('cat-arena-on');
    caption(arenaCaption(arena.claimed.length, arena.total));
    const now = performance.now();
    arena.openedAt = now;
    arena.movedAt = now;
    arena.spokeAt = now;
    arena.spent = 0;
    arena.interrupts = 0;
    arena.lastLine = '';
    arena.ending = null;
    scrub.seen = now;
    scrub.target = null;
    paintTerritory(behindCurtain);
    arena.ammoWas = [
      ...document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('#cat-score .cat-paw[data-slug]'),
    ].map((p) => p.classList.contains('got'));
    takeCat(now);
    arena.raf = requestAnimationFrame(tick);
  }

  /**
   * Borrow the cat from `SiteCat.astro` and start it wherever it already is.
   *
   * Starting from its real position rather than a corner matters: the fight opens
   * with the cat where the visitor last saw it, so the boss is recognisably the
   * same animal that was wandering the footer a second ago.
   */
  function takeCat(now: number): void {
    document.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent('cat:standdown'));
    const root = catEl();
    if (!root) return;
    root.classList.add('boss');
    // SiteCat's `stop()` parked it as `idle`; a boss is not idle, and leaving the
    // class on lets its resting animations fight the phase ones. `grooming` goes for the
    // same reason and one more: SiteCat may have left one running from its own idle
    // repertoire, and a fight that opens mid-wash would inherit it.
    root.classList.remove('idle');
    endGrooming(root);
    const r = root.getBoundingClientRect();
    boss.last = now;
    boss.facing = 1;
    boss.stance = pickStance(arena.seed);
    boss.feint = false;
    boss.telegraphMul = 1;
    boss.slowUntil = 0;
    boss.slowMul = 1;
    boss.anchorUntil = 0;
    boss.anchorPx = 0;
    boss.lureMs = LURE_MS;
    boss.immuneMs = LURE_MS;
    boss.regrowAt = now + STANCES[pickStance(arena.seed)].regrowMs;
    // Every fight starts even, whatever the last one ended as: `mood` is hysteretic, so a
    // stale tier would carry the previous board's verdict into the opening of this one.
    boss.mood = 'even';
    boss.groomedAt = now;
    root.dataset.mood = 'even';
    root.dataset.stance = boss.stance;
    bossPhase(root, 'stalk', now);
    placeCat(root, r.left + r.width / 2, r.top + r.height / 2);
  }

  /** Give it back, from where the fight left it, and let it walk on. */
  function releaseCat(): void {
    const root = catEl();
    if (root) {
      root.classList.remove(
        'boss',
        'boss-telegraph',
        'boss-leap',
        'boss-recover',
        'boss-eat',
        'boss-fetch',
        'boss-feint',
      );
      endGrooming(root);
      delete root.dataset.stance;
      delete root.dataset.mood;
      document.dispatchEvent(
        new CustomEvent('cat:resume', { detail: { x: boss.x - root.offsetWidth / 2 } }),
      );
    } else {
      document.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent('cat:resume'));
    }
  }

  /**
   * End it, with the DOM exactly as found. Pillar 2 — and the reason Esc, the
   * toggle, a cleared board and the auto-truce all come through here.
   */
  function close(): void {
    cancelAnimationFrame(arena.raf);
    arena.raf = 0;
    for (const node of arena.claimed) free(node, false);
    arena.claimed = [];
    arena.freed = [];
    arena.order = [];
    arena.total = 0;
    arena.on = false;
    /*
     * `want` is deliberately *not* touched here. It is intent, owned by `setArena` and by
     * `open`'s no-board bail; this function is mechanism. Syncing it looked like harmless
     * belt-and-braces and was the opposite: pressing the toggle during the exit transition
     * runs `setArena(true)`, which aborts the out-curtain and delivers its `close()` — so
     * `close()` would land *after* the new intent and reset it to false. The button then read
     * "off" over a fight that was opening, and the next press opened a second board on top of
     * the first, stranding the first one's `--claim-tilt` on the page.
     */
    document.documentElement.classList.remove('cat-arena-on');
    hideRing();
    scrub.target = null;
    scrub.x = scrub.y = -1;
    scrub.vx = scrub.vy = 0;
    clearTreat();
    hideTerritory();
    releaseCat();
    arena.caption = '';
    arena.order = [];
    restoreAmmo();
    restoreCaption();
  }

  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------- *
   * The toggle (§13)
   * ---------------------------------------------------------------- */

  const toggle = () => el('cat-arena-toggle') as HTMLButtonElement | null;

  /**
   * Why the arena can't open right now, or null if it can.
   *
   * Both answers are "not yet", not "not for you", and both are stated in the
   * widget rather than by an absent button — §11's reversal, applied to the
   * second case as well as the first.
   */
  function blockedBecause(): string | null {
    if (motionReduced()) return 'Not while motion is reduced — there is no still version of this yet.';
    // The core verb is *holding a cursor still on a thing*. A touch screen has no
    // hover to hold, so on a phone this would be a button that starts a game you
    // cannot play. Saying so beats hiding it and beats faking it.
    if (!matchMedia('(hover: hover) and (pointer: fine)').matches) {
      return 'Needs a mouse or trackpad — the touch version isn’t designed yet.';
    }
    return null;
  }

  /**
   * Make the button agree with reality — every time either changes.
   *
   * The failure state §13.5 names is a button whose rendering and the arena's
   * actual state disagree, which is why this is called from `setArena` rather
   * than from the click handler: an auto-truce or a cleared board flips the
   * button back on its own, visibly, instead of leaving it pressed over a dead
   * arena.
   */
  function syncToggle(): void {
    const btn = toggle();
    if (!btn) return;
    btn.setAttribute('aria-pressed', arena.want ? 'true' : 'false');
    const why = blockedBecause();
    btn.setAttribute('aria-disabled', why ? 'true' : 'false');
    const note = el('cat-arena-note');
    if (note) {
      if (why) note.textContent = why;
      note.hidden = !why;
    }
    // Describe the button only while there is a reason to; a description that is
    // always attached but usually irrelevant is noise on every focus.
    if (why) btn.setAttribute('aria-describedby', 'cat-arena-note');
    else btn.removeAttribute('aria-describedby');
  }

  function init(): void {
    const btn = toggle();
    if (!btn || btn.dataset.init) return;
    btn.dataset.init = '1';

    // Revealing the toggle here is what stops it being a dead control: if this
    // component is ever dropped from the layout, the button never appears.
    btn.closest<HTMLElement>('.cat-arena-chip')?.removeAttribute('hidden');

    btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
      if (blockedBecause()) {
        // `aria-disabled`, not `disabled`, so the press is ours to answer: the
        // note is already showing, so flash it rather than doing nothing at all.
        const note = el('cat-arena-note');
        if (!note) return;
        note.classList.remove('cat-arena-note-nudge');
        void note.offsetWidth; // reflow, or re-adding the class re-runs nothing
        note.classList.add('cat-arena-note-nudge');
        return;
      }
      // The toggle is the same gesture as Esc, so it gets the same parting line.
      if (arena.want) truce();
      else setArena(true);
    });

    window.addEventListener(
      'pointermove',
      (e) => {
        if (!arena.on) return;
        const now = performance.now();
        const dt = (now - scrub.at) / 1000;
        /*
         * Velocity from one pair of samples, and only over an interval short enough to
         * mean anything. A gap measured in whole seconds — the first move of a fight, or
         * one after the tab was away — says nothing about how fast the cursor is going
         * now.
         *
         * Outside that window the velocity is **zeroed, not kept.** Keeping the last
         * good reading looked more careful and was a bug with teeth: a cursor creeping a
         * pixel at a time still refreshes `scrub.at`, so the aim was led by a velocity
         * sampled from some flick seconds earlier and the cat missed by up to
         * `PREDICT_CAP` every single time. The harness found it as a fight that could not
         * be lost — the cat pounced for seventy seconds and never once landed. Not
         * knowing the speed has to mean assuming there isn't one.
         */
        if (scrub.at && dt > 0.004 && dt < 0.12) {
          scrub.vx = (e.clientX - scrub.x) / dt;
          scrub.vy = (e.clientY - scrub.y) / dt;
        } else {
          scrub.vx = 0;
          scrub.vy = 0;
        }
        scrub.at = now;
        scrub.x = e.clientX;
        scrub.y = e.clientY;
        scrub.inside = true;
        scrub.seen = now;
      },
      { passive: true },
    );

    /*
     * Click to throw (§3, §5.4).
     *
     * Anything the page uses is left alone: a click on something `INTERACTIVE` — a link, a
     * button, a field — does what it always did, because §11 promises the page stays
     * usable and pillar 2 promises a recruiter can keep reading. So does a click in
     * the cat's own HUD, which is where the toggle lives. Everything else is a throw.
     *
     * `INTERACTIVE` and not `PROTECTED`: the latter includes `[tabindex]`, which matches
     * `<main tabindex="-1">` — so using it here meant no click inside the page content ever
     * threw anything, while the crosshair said it would. See the note in `arena.ts`.
     *
     * Not `preventDefault`ed anywhere: there is nothing to prevent on a paragraph,
     * and swallowing the event would take text selection with it.
     */
    window.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
      if (!arena.on) return;
      const t = e.target;
      if (t instanceof Element && (t.closest(INTERACTIVE) || t.closest('#cat-hud'))) return;
      throwTreat(e.clientX, e.clientY);
    });

    // The pointer leaving the window is not the same as the pointer holding still
    // (§5.3). `pointerout` with a null relatedTarget is the one that fires when it
    // leaves the document rather than merely crossing between elements.
    document.addEventListener('pointerout', (e) => {
      if (!(e as PointerEvent).relatedTarget) {
        scrub.inside = false;
        scrub.vx = scrub.vy = 0;
        hideRing();
      }
    });
    document.addEventListener('pointerover', () => (scrub.inside = true));

    // Esc, and only Esc: the truce keystroke has to work from anywhere, and the
    // arena adds no other binding (§11 — the site's own shortcuts stay owned by
    // the pages that define them).
    window.addEventListener('keydown', (e) => {
      // `want`, not `on`: Esc during the flood is the clearest case there is of an
      // interruption that must not have to wait for an animation to finish.
      if (e.key === 'Escape' && arena.want) truce();
    });

    // Tab hidden long enough ends it: nobody comes back to a page mid-invasion.
    let hiddenAt = 0;
    document.addEventListener('visibilitychange', () => {
      if (document.hidden) {
        hiddenAt = Date.now();
        return;
      }
      if (arena.on && hiddenAt && Date.now() - hiddenAt > HIDDEN_TRUCE_MS) endQuietly();
    });

    // Reduce-motion can be flipped mid-visit from either source (the OS query or
    // the site's own toggle). Turning it on ends a running fight, because there
    // is no still version of this yet.
    const reconsider = () => {
      if (arena.on && blockedBecause()) endQuietly();
      else syncToggle();
    };
    matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').addEventListener('change', reconsider);
    new MutationObserver(reconsider).observe(document.documentElement, {
      attributes: true,
      attributeFilter: ['class'],
    });

    syncToggle();
  }

  document.addEventListener('astro:page-load', () => {
    init();
    // A curtain caught mid-flight by a navigation has nothing left to reveal.
    arena.curtain?.();
    arena.curtain = null;
    stopCurtain();
    // A fight does not survive a page change yet: the old board's elements are
    // gone, and step 1 has nothing worth carrying across. Re-querying the new
    // page (§13.6) waits for the build that has something to carry.
    if (arena.on) endQuietly();
    syncToggle();
  });
</script>
